Dear Members of the SI Peer Review Committee,

Kindly accept this letter and submitted materials as my application for third-year reappointment as Assistant Professor in the School of Information at Pratt Institute. In this packet, you will find my up-to-date curriculum vitae, along with documents detailing my contributions to research, teaching, and service since I joined Pratt in Fall 2024.

Since joining the School of Information, I have developed a research agenda at the intersection of the humanities and computer science, using digital and computational methods to study language, gender, sex, and sexuality. In my teaching, I have developed courses that bring together humanistic approaches to interpretation with programming, data science, machine learning, and digital humanities methods. I have also contributed to Pratt through student advising, mentoring, and participation in the intellectual and professional life of the School. More details about these activities are provided in the accompanying research and teaching statements. Here, I would like to highlight several accomplishments from my first two years at Pratt.

**Teaching**

Since Fall 2024, I have taught five courses across the School of Information: INFO 601: Foundations of Information; INFO 664: Programming Cultural Heritage; INFO 657: Digital Humanities; INFO 697: Special Topics—How to Build a Bot; and INFO 656: Machine Learning. Across these courses, I have worked to develop a teaching practice that brings together computational and humanistic skills, with particular attention to close reading, writing, and the contingent nature of knowledge.

In my programming and computational courses, I emphasize that technical tools are not simply neutral instruments, but objects that can themselves be examined, interpreted, and questioned. In INFO 664, for example, students work with programming, data, and computational methods while also attending closely to the cultural and interpretive dimensions of the materials they analyze. Student projects have included the use of Small Language Models to examine gender bias in popular culture, toxic online spaces, and sensationalist language. Three projects from the course received the 2026 Info Show's People's Choice Award.

I similarly use writing as an active learning practice across my courses. In INFO 656: Machine Learning, students complete a conference-style paper that asks them to situate their computational work within an emerging field of research. In INFO 601 and INFO 657, I use writing workshops and interdisciplinary readings to help students develop their capacity to formulate and communicate critical arguments. Student evaluations have consistently highlighted the welcoming classroom environments I strive to create, as well as my ability to make complex technical and theoretical material accessible to students with different backgrounds.

I have also developed new curricular opportunities in programming and computational practice. In Summer 2025, I taught INFO 697: How to Build a Bot, a course I proposed at Pratt that combines hands-on programming with critical examination of coding, automation, and the social implications of bots. I plan to continue developing this area of the curriculum through a Python course focused specifically on working with text-based data, building on a workshop I currently offer through the Data Storytelling Lab.

In addition to classroom teaching, I currently advise approximately 35 students across LIS, Data Analytics and Visualization, and Information Experience Design. I meet individually with students to discuss coursework, projects, portfolios, and career goals, and I advise the Stacks Student Journal and Latines in Tech. These activities have allowed me to extend my teaching and mentorship beyond individual courses and to contribute to students' broader intellectual and professional development.

**Research**

My research since joining Pratt has established a sustained interdisciplinary agenda connecting queer and trans studies, digital humanities, computational linguistics, and AI ethics. As a language scholar trained in English literature, queer studies, and digital humanities, I use computational tools to examine how language related to gender, sex, and sexuality is represented, processed, and transformed through information technologies. A central concern of this work is understanding how computational mechanisms shape what can be seen, represented, and said in language data.

From Fall 2024 through Fall 2026, I have written five papers, two of which have been published in peer-reviewed venues and three of which are currently under review. My published work includes “Some Myths About Bias: A Queer Studies Reading of Gender Bias in NLP,” published in the Association for Computational Linguistics Proceedings in 2025, and “Re-encoding Dominance: Queer Approaches to TEI Markup,” published in Digital Editing & Publishing in the 21st Century. Together, these projects demonstrate my commitment to bringing humanistic approaches into computational and information research while also using computational technologies as objects of critical inquiry.

My current work extends this agenda in several directions. “Small Language Models and the Synthetic Middle,” under review at FAccT 2027, uses computational experiments and close reading to examine linguistic overlap between polarized discourses on gender and transgender rights. “What LLMs Can't See About Gender,” also under review at FAccT 2027, draws on trans studies to examine the limitations of existing approaches to evaluating gender bias in large language models. A third paper, “‘My Body Feels Like It's Coming Off’: On Cis Embodiment in Love Is Blind,” currently under review at Transgender Studies Quarterly, brings computational and close-reading approaches to contemporary popular culture and questions of gender and embodiment.

My book project, *Queer Code: Re-working Language Technologies*, is under contract with Bloomsbury Academic Press, with the manuscript due in March 2027. The book examines technologies including web scraping, text analysis, markup languages, screen display, and machine learning, asking how these technologies reduce the complexity of language in order to make it computationally manageable. Drawing on concepts from queer studies, the book develops alternative ways of reading and working with computational systems and language data.

Across these projects, I have sought to communicate across fields that do not always share methods or assumptions. My publications in computational linguistics, AI ethics, digital scholarly editing, trans studies, and digital humanities reflect this commitment. This interdisciplinary orientation is also central to my role as a faculty member in the School of Information: my research treats information technologies not only as tools for analysis, but as systems that actively shape how knowledge, categories, and meanings become visible.

**Service and Future Contributions**

In addition to my research and teaching, I have contributed to the School of Information through student mentoring and advising and through service to Pratt and broader scholarly communities. I have also participated in the Pratt Faculty Union and external scholarly communities, as detailed in my curriculum vitae and accompanying materials.

Looking ahead, I intend to continue building connections among computational practice, critical information studies, and the humanities. My immediate research priority is completing *Queer Code*, while continuing my work on contemporary ideological discourse around gender and the implications of language technologies. In teaching, I plan to develop new opportunities for students to work with text-based data and programming, as well as a seminar in queer studies that would combine theoretical study with visits to queer archives in New York City. I am also exploring, with colleagues, the development of a study-abroad program in Lisbon focused on the intersection of digital humanities and archival work.

Taken together, my work at Pratt has allowed me to develop a coherent program of teaching, research, and mentorship centered on the critical study of information technologies and the forms of knowledge they produce. I am grateful for the opportunity to contribute to the School of Information and look forward to continuing to develop this work as a member of the Pratt faculty.

Thank you for your consideration of my application.

